    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d155">

                <pb facs="0288" n="246" sameAs="frus:frus1863p1/pg246"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr. Adams.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 549.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <hi
                                rendition="#italic">Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1863-04-13"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >April</hi> 13, 1863.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> You will find the newspaper reports from
                    the west quite confused. The War Department has regular advices, and is
                    confident of ultimate and not long delayed success at Vicksburg.</p>
                <p>The late reconnoissance at Charleston is regarded by the navy as establishing the
                    invulnerability of the monitors, and, of course, their ability to reduce the
                    land fortifications at that place. The only obstacle now remaining to be
                    overcome is the obstructions in the channel. The attention of the fleet is now
                    engaged upon this point.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles Francis Adams</hi>, Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c., &amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
